Original commit body from f44354466e6ddaac23de48560a3bfea33ef04a7b:

`--json` is registered on the tracker's top-level parser, ahead of
`add_subparsers`, so `scripts/tracker --json show <ID>` works and the trailing
`scripts/tracker show <ID> --json` exits 2.

AGENTS.md:82 — the always-loaded governance text every agent reads before
anything else — documented the trailing form. LIVE.md's landmine list carried
the correct form, so the two contradicted each other and the wrong one was
read first.

**Why this was worth more than a typo fix.** Both ways of getting the
invocation wrong (flag position, and running the CLI outside the repo root)
write their diagnostic to stderr and leave stdout empty. A caller that pipes
stdout into a JSON parser and discards stderr therefore cannot distinguish
"you invoked the CLI wrong" from "that item does not exist". Two consecutive
sessions produced full columns of false `UNRESOLVED` that way — a 13-ID sweep
and a 42-ID sweep.

## What changed

Six documentation surfaces, all the same defect:

- `AGENTS.md:82` — the hand-maintained copy (it is *not* inside the managed
  block, so `htrac setup` was not the thing rewriting it).
- `packages/hypergumbo-tracker/src/hypergumbo_tracker/setup.py` —
  `MANAGED_BLOCK_CONTENT`, which `htrac setup` injects into **adopting repos'**
  AGENTS.md. Same wrong form, shipped outward.
- `docs/adr/0013-structured-tracker.md` — three occurrences.
- `.agent/.../tracker-hygiene-dedup-meta-analysis-playbook.md` — four, one of
  which is a **runnable** `./scripts/tracker list --json > corpus.json`. That
  would have exited 2 and left an empty `corpus.json` behind, which the sweep's
  workers then read as an empty tracker.
- `.agent/hooks/_shared/on_transcript_change.py` — the playbook summary the
  hook injects into agent context.

## The guard

`tests/test_tracker_json_flag_documented.py`, in the shape of the existing
`test_hg_github_token_documented.py` rot-guard (pure file reads, no source
import, so it neither contributes nor consumes package coverage):

1. **Anchor** — `--json` is still registered exactly once, ahead of
   `add_subparsers`. If WI-dolaf's structural half lands and the subparsers
   start accepting `--json`, the trailing form becomes valid; this test then
   fails and says to relax the guard, rather than the guard silently becoming
   wrong.
2. **Forbid** — no documentation surface shows the trailing form. Scanning is
   per **backtick code span**, not per line: prose explaining the hazard names
   both orders in one sentence, and a line-level regex cannot tell that from a
   real bad invocation. (I hit exactly that and had to reword AGENTS.md so it
   states the rule without quoting the broken literal.)
3. **Require** — AGENTS.md positively shows the working form. A forbid-only
   guard is satisfiable by deleting the documentation.

**The guard earned itself immediately**: it found a third ADR-0013 occurrence
that two hand greps had reported clean. The second sweep piped through
`grep -v '\.ops'` to drop tracker op-log paths — and the offending line's own
text ends `...files ending in \`.ops\`.`, so the noise filter deleted the
finding and the sweep exited 0 printing nothing. 3 matches unfiltered, 2
filtered.

## Scope

Documentation only; no behaviour change. WI-dolaf stays **open** on its
structural half — accepting the trailing form as an argparse alias, and/or
emitting a JSON error object on stdout so a parser sees `{"error": ...}`
instead of empty input. That is the fix that removes the hazard rather than
documenting around it, and it is not in this PR.

Not fixed here, filed separately: the same `MANAGED_BLOCK_CONTENT` documents
`holding` (deprecated) and `wontfix` (never a status; the resolved set is
`done` / `wont_do` / `deleted`). Different mechanism — stale enum, not flag
position.

